<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pratapgad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1656">1656</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pratapgad is a hill fort in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in 1656 on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> to guard the passes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>. Below its walls in November 1659 Shivaji killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> general Afzal Khan, and his forces then defeated the Bijapuri army. The fort became a symbol of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> power and contains a temple of the goddess Bhavani.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pratapgad.jpg" alt="Pratapgad" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neeraj Rane · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pratapgad_The_Fort_of_Valour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
